            <ArticleTitle>Primary epithelioid hemangioendothelioma of the lumbar spine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>385-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.spinee.2006.10.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Primary epitheliod hemangioendotheliomas of the lumbar spine are extremely rare tumors with an unpredictable clinical course.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a case of primary lumbar epithelioid hemangioendothelioma and its treatment protocol.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN/SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A tertiary Spinal Unit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT SAMPLE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A previously healthy adult with persisting low back pain and neurologic deficit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The diagnosis was confirmed by core needle biopsy. The patient was treated with preoperative embolization followed by staged surgery which included wide tumor resection and spinal fixation, using a cage system and a titanium plate anteriorly and a transpedicular fixation system posteriorly.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Three years postoperatively the patient remains free of symptoms with no evidence of recurrence.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The treatment of these unpredictable tumors should be dictated by adherence to general oncologic principles.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
